44
00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:58.479
Richard Bair: But the new fees that Josh is implementing, the fees hip, has a component in there where we define what the penalty is for due diligence failures.

45
00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:01.429
Richard Bair: So it'll be part of the fee computation.

46
00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:10.659
Alex Popowycz: Yeah, I'm just trying to think of, in this particular case, if the…

47
00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:19.920
Alex Popowycz: Node account… what is specifically a node account… what penalties would a node account be paying?

48
00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.690
Joseph Sinclair (Hashgraph Foundry Inc. (DBA Hashgraph)): It pays the network portion of the fees.

49
00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:31.280
Alex Popowycz: But…

50
00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:32.810
Joseph Sinclair (Hashgraph Foundry Inc. (DBA Hashgraph)): And a due diligence failure.

51
00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:40.850
Richard Bair: Or due diligence failures is where the fees come out of these things. So if a node were to, you know, submit a transaction…

52
00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.699
Richard Bair: That wasn't properly signed, for example.

53
00:10:45.580 --> 00:11:01.740
Alex Popowycz: Got it, got it. But… so that fee is… so that penalty is a placeholder for the new fee structure that defines these penalties. Like, if you have, like, a decent RPC relay into the node, and it pre… doesn't do the pre-check properly.

54
00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:09.510
Alex Popowycz: Submits it and it fails, due to a pre-check failure, then the node itself is

55
00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.580
Alex Popowycz: Penalized for that action.

56
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:14.840
Richard Bair: Correct.

57
00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:24.299
Richard Bair: In some cases, for some due diligence failures, they just pay for the network fee, and don't get a node fee.

58
00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:29.369
Richard Bair: And in other cases where it's something really egregious, then there's a…

59
00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:33.460
Richard Bair: a punitive fee that is associated with it. So, for example.

60
00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:48.959
Richard Bair: It's possible that in a… in the normal flow of events, at the time that you did pre-check, the transaction was properly signed, but there was another transaction in… in flight that changes the keys on the account. By the time…

61
00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:58.669
Richard Bair: transaction that you tested at pre-check that was accurate gets to actual consensus, it's no longer properly signed. In that case, the node still has to pay for that.

62
00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:12.850
Richard Bair: the network fee portion, but it's not punitive, because, you know, it wasn't really their fault, probably. Although you can't tell, you can't tell, so you give them the benefit of the doubt. On the other side of it, you have

63
00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:22.169
Richard Bair: a transaction that cannot be parsed at all. If you get an event from a node, and you're trying to parse, you know, protobuf parse the transactions, and they don't even parse.

64
00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:38.279
Richard Bair: there is no excuse for that. If somebody's doing that to you, they are literally attacking you, so you can charge a punitive fee for that. So there's different fees depending on the particular due diligence failure, but in all cases, it's a due diligence failure that causes them to have to actually pay

65
00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.080
Richard Bair: funds from that, Node account.

